          "name": "README — how to decode the JWT",
          "request": {
            "method": "GET",
            "header": [],
            "url": {
              "raw": "{{base_url}}",
              "host": ["{{base_url}}"]
            },
            "description": "After running 'Login — happy path', the test script saves the JWT into the collection variable 'last_jwt'.\n\nTo inspect its contents:\n1. Open Postman → collection variables → copy the value of 'last_jwt'\n2. Go to https://jwt.io\n3. Paste into the 'Encoded' box on the left\n4. The decoded payload on the right will show:\n   {\n     \"sub\": \"1\",              ← user ID in our DB\n     \"email\": \"alice@example.com\",\n     \"username\": \"alice\",\n     \"roles\": [\"ROLE_USER\"],\n     \"iss\": \"link\",\n     \"iat\": 1714950000,       ← issued at (Unix timestamp)\n     \"exp\": 1714950900,       ← expires at (iat + 900 seconds)\n     \"jti\": \"c2a1b3d4-...\"   ← unique token ID\n   }\n\nThe signature will show 'Invalid Signature' on jwt.io because it needs the RSA public key.\nThat is expected and correct — the key is not public."
          }
        }
      ]
    }
